WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.503 --> 00:00:11.920
DAMITH KARUNARATNE:
Hey, everyone.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.580
Welcome to the session
on CI/CD anywhere.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.730
In this session, we'll be
discussing why CI/CD anywhere

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.820
matters and how we're helping
customers streamline their

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.000
build, test, and deploy
processes across cloud

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.840
and on-premise environments.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.840
My name is Damith Karunaratne,
and I'm a Senior Product

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.550
Manager at Google Cloud.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.750
And this is my
colleague, Ben Houston,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.650
who is a Kubernetes
Specialist at Google Cloud.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:39.800
Today, we will be speaking to
you about Google's new approach

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.200
to doing CI/CD anywhere.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.390
Before I dive into
product details,

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.460
I'd like to turn it over to Ben.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.140
He's going to give
a quick overview

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.240
on why a hybrid
approach to CI/CD

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.520
is such a hot topic these days.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.830
BEN HUSTON: Damith, thanks
for the great introduction.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:58.330
And to those of you
in the audience,

00:00:58.330 --> 00:00:59.892
I have an important
question for you.

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:01.600
Why should we care
about being able to do

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.491
continuous integration and
continuous delivery anywhere?

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.780
Well, we should care
because CI/CD flexibility

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.013
is an extremely important
topic right now.

00:01:12.013 --> 00:01:13.680
This is due to the
fact that enterprises

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.350
need build processes that
can keep pace with constantly

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.530
changing business, regulatory,
and security requirements

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.440
and which will work across
multiple environments

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.840
they have made massive
investments in.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.540
More specifically, enterprises
need transformational

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.970
development capabilities,
like build anywhere or deploy

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.240
anywhere, because these can
support massive on-demand

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.390
scaleup, are inherently
resilient and highly available,

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:38.640
can be authored and managed
in a completely versioned,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.310
declarative manner, and
are instrumented with

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.650
out-of-the-box tooling
which provides end-to-end

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.820
observability across
the entire build system.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.800
Unfortunately, most
enterprises are still

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.720
using flexible legacy
build tools like Jenkins,

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.330
which predate the cloud.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:55.923
These tools simply can't meet
enterprise's current needs,

00:01:55.923 --> 00:01:57.590
because they don't
provide a uniform way

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.470
to do continuous integration
or continuous delivery

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.880
across different environments.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.780
And they also can't easily
scale to accommodate

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.690
the type of extremely high
volume, high concurrency

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:13.930
CI/CD that dominates modern
software development.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.810
The good news is that most
of the performance problems,

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:19.140
which have traditionally
plagued build systems,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.720
have been solved in recent
years by Kubernetes, which

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.460
is an extremely
popular orchestration

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:27.150
framework for operating
systems at massive scale.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.490
The most highly
sought-after at-scale system

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:33.000
attributes, such as flexibility,
scalability, resilience,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.690
control, and performance, are
now provided out of the box

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.360
by the Kubernetes API,
its unique approach

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.493
to distribute application
lifecycle management.

00:02:42.493 --> 00:02:44.660
Next-gen build tools, like
the one that my colleague

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.420
Damith will introduce next,
understand they do not

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.220
need to reinvent the wheel.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:52.610
These tools explicitly harness
the power of the Kubernetes API

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.250
and extend it with the rich,
new primitives specifically

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:59.842
designed to address the most
difficult CI/CD use cases.

00:02:59.842 --> 00:03:02.050
Now, let me explain why
Kubernetes is such a big deal

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.610
for modern CI/CD.

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:05.320
Let me turn the
floor back to Damith

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.230
to introduce Google's new
approach to CI/CD anywhere.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.670
DAMITH KARUNARATNE: Thanks, Ben.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:14.270
With all of this in mind, I'd
like to introduce Cloud Build

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:15.720
Hybrid.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.060
With Cloud Build
Hybrid, Google Cloud

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.360
is taking the power,
flexibility, and simplicity

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.820
of Google Cloud Build and
bringing it to anywhere

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:27.300
you can run Kubernetes.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.570
Cloud Build Hybrid is powered
by Tekton, which means out

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.790
of the box, you
avoid vendor lock-in

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:34.260
and get the
reliability that you've

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.200
come to expect from Kubernetes.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.140
Capabilities like self-healing
and graceful failover

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.940
allow for no single
point of failure.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.310
Being able to run directly
on your infrastructure

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.410
behind your firewalls
now means you

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.020
can get the advantages
of cloud-native CI/CD

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:54.230
for workloads that can't
easily be moved to the cloud.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.820
But Cloud Build
Hybrid is not only

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:58.820
about providing the benefits
of Kubernetes in Tekton.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.700
Since this is an extension
of Google Cloud Build,

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.370
Cloud Build Hybrid provides
you a consistent interface

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.150
and workflow for building and
deploying your applications.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.910
You can seamlessly switch
between using the fully managed

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.400
or hybrid version of
Cloud Build without having

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.200
to change your workflow.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.060
You also get a
single pane of glass

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.010
through the existing
Cloud Build interface,

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.870
so you can easily
see activity across

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.480
all your CI/CD
environments in one place,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.570
regardless of whether you're
using the fully managed

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.200
or hybrid version.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.840
With Cloud Build's
developer-centric syntax,

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.210
you're able to easily
define your CI/CD workloads

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.850
without having advanced
knowledge of Kubernetes.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.590
So developers can
spend more time

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.450
on building and
delivering software

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:44.110
that drives the business.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.900
And upgrades have
never been easier.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.240
All Cloud Build Hybrid instances
can be centrally managed,

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.870
which includes automated
updates and patches to make

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.430
sure you're always secure.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:58.820
In addition to this, with the
Google-managed control plane,

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:02.330
you get the availability and
support your business relies on

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:03.920
to ensure smooth operation.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:09.110
With Cloud Build Hybrid,
we handle the complexity

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:11.690
of multienvironment CI/CD.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:13.400
Just add environment
configuration

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.530
to your Cloud Build
YAML, and your pipelines

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:17.570
will easily and
transparently run

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:20.450
the workload against registered
Kubernetes environments,

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.230
regardless of where
they're located.

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:25.240
Scaling these workloads
across your environment

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.190
is also simplified.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.130
Cloud Build Hybrid harnesses
the power of Kubernetes

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:32.950
to provide robust
autoscaling and concurrency

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:35.910
for all your CI/CD processes.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.100
Not only is scaling
simplified but it's

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.770
also more efficient
with resource pooling.

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:43.800
All workloads are entirely
containerized and managed using

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.250
Kubernetes, allowing them to
be scheduled right alongside

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.190
non-CI/CD workloads.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.770
Workloads also run
completely hermetically,

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:55.230
ensuring that no build
time dependencies ever

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:58.500
cross-contaminate the runtime
dependencies relied upon

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.290
by other colocated workloads.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.480
For hard-to-update,
resource-constrained,

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.480
on-premise environments
this scheduling fungibility

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.830
is extremely valuable.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:14.320
Now, let's take a peek under
the hood to see how this works.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.870
On the left, we have Google
Cloud in the fully managed

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:18.730
control plane.

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:21.640
On the right, we have a
hybrid multicloud environment

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:24.640
running a Kubernetes cluster.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.830
With Google Cloud's
Connect service,

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:29.470
we can connect a Kubernetes
cluster to the Google Cloud

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:31.260
control plane.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.390
Doing this will install
the Connect agent,

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:35.490
which will allow the
control plane to communicate

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.770
with the cluster.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:39.720
Once the cluster
is connected, we

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.250
can enable the
Cloud Build feature

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.840
and install hybrid worker pools.

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:46.550
These hybrid worker
pools consist

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:48.980
of Tekton and some
additional components

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:52.010
and configuration that make
interacting with Google Cloud

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:54.240
seamless.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.150
Once hybrid worker
pools are installed,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:00.180
Cloud IAM will need to be
configured for Cloud Build

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.010
service accounts to
properly communicate

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.100
with the worker pool.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.020
And finally, we'll
need to make sure

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.390
that the Kubernetes service
account on the worker pool

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:11.150
is configured to interact with
our source control and artifact

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:12.080
repositories.

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:16.770
Once Cloud Build
Hybrid is set up,

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:18.710
you can leverage the
same Cloud Build workflow

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:22.340
as the fully managed offering
to build, test, and deploy

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:24.360
your applications.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:26.220
Your workload is
executed as a series

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:27.900
of build steps,
which are defined

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.890
as containers we call builders.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.890
Since each build step is
hermetic and only exists

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:36.110
for the duration of
the step, any data

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:37.850
that you wish to
share across steps

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:41.470
can be shared through
a common workspace.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:44.470
Google Cloud provides a set of
fully supported and maintained

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:46.810
builders for a
variety of workloads,

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.360
but there are also over a
hundred community builders

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:50.800
available.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:52.320
You can also create
your own builder

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:56.040
to address unique use cases.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.590
This workflow can
not only be used

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:01.010
to build containers but also
noncontainers, such as Java,

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:03.155
or Go artifacts, or
golden VM images.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.560
The Cloud Build syntax
remains the same

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.560
for executing workloads
on hybrid worker pools.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:13.840
All you need to do is specify
the hybrid pool that's

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.240
going to execute the workload.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:18.730
For instance, in this
"Hello, world" example,

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.910
which has six lines of code,
we specify the hybrid pool

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:23.920
that the build is
going to run on.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:26.800
This build will be transformed
into a Tekton task run

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:28.840
before it's sent to
the hybrid worker pool

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:32.990
with optional support for
Docker and Docker builds.

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.210
There's a large community
that uses Docker today,

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.460
and we want to make sure the
experience for the Docker

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.140
community is as
seamless as possible.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:41.870
This means they
don't have to worry

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:43.460
about the overhead
of orchestrating

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:46.130
Docker and Docker build
processes in Kubernetes

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:48.890
since we take care of this
based on the hybrid pool

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.510
configuration.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:54.700
The resulting Tekton
task run in this example,

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.830
which executes a
Docker and Docker build

00:08:56.830 --> 00:09:00.460
ends up being 156 lines.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:04.870
This translates to roughly
26 times more lines of code.

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:07.150
We're taking care of the
boilerplate and Kubernetes

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:10.690
configuration required to run
this build while integrating it

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:14.620
with Google Cloud Services, such
as Cloud IAM, Cloud Logging,

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.180
and source control integrations.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:20.240
This allows developers to focus
on building great software

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.640
and not worry about the
underlying technologies running

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:28.070
their CI/CD workloads across
all their environments.

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:29.920
Cloud Build and
Cloud Build Hybrid

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:33.040
are part of a much broader
end-to-end GCP DevOps tool

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:33.970
chain.

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:36.010
Some of these
platforms and services

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:39.370
will be highlighted in the demo
that Ben is about to show you.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.695
Ben, over to you.

00:09:41.695 --> 00:09:42.820
BEN HUSTON: Thanks, Damith.

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:44.607
Awesome product overview.

00:09:44.607 --> 00:09:46.690
I'm going to quickly walk
through the logical flow

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:49.020
of a short demo we've prepared.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.720
First, we'll see a
developer check new code

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.580
into version control.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:57.300
Next, we'll see Cloud Build
Hybrid detect the code change

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:59.610
and begin to kick off builds.

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:02.880
Then we'll see four
processes concurrently

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:05.070
building and deploying
the same application

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:07.270
to four different
target environments--

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:10.800
GKE, Anthos attached
clusters in AWS and Azure,

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.860
and an Anthos bare-metal cluster
which is running on premise.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:17.790
Finally, we'll see the overall
build progress being monitored

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:20.430
with built-in tooling, like
the Cloud Operations suite

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.800
and Cloud Build dashboards.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.150
OK, on onto the demo.

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:29.730
All right, you're now ready
for a quick demo of Cloud Build

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:30.785
Hybrid.

00:10:30.785 --> 00:10:32.160
Here, we see four
different types

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.202
of Kubernetes clusters
registered with the Google

00:10:34.202 --> 00:10:35.780
Cloud control plane--

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:40.370
GKE cluster, two Anthos-attached
clusters for EKS and AKS,

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:43.590
and Anthos bare-metal cluster
that is running on premise.

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:45.810
The great news is that
once you register a cluster

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:47.950
to Google Cloud,
installing Cloud Build

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:49.140
Hybrid it's dead simple.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.750
Now we're going to open
a Cloud Shell session

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:58.180
and run a quick CLI command
to confirm that our cluster is

00:10:58.180 --> 00:10:58.900
ready for action.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.310
Yep, the install [INAUDIBLE]
is OK across the board.

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:16.858
OK, now that the environment
overview is out of the way,

00:11:16.858 --> 00:11:19.400
let's quickly explain how Cloud
Build Hybrid jobs get queued.

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:23.900
Cloud Build Hybrid
uses a configuration

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:26.390
construct called the trigger
to associate a build event

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:28.910
like a pull request
with a build pipeline

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:31.100
and kick start
the build process.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:33.800
You can see we have four
triggers to find, one for each

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:35.593
of our Kubernetes clusters.

00:11:35.593 --> 00:11:37.010
In a moment, we're
going to switch

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:39.290
to an IDE, a.k.a., an
Interactive Development

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:40.490
Environment.

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:43.550
We'll commit a config change
and push it to our Git repo.

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:45.473
Afterwards, the
triggers we defined here

00:11:45.473 --> 00:11:47.390
will listen for this
commit, and they kick off

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:49.880
a Cloud Build Hybrid job for
each of our environments.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:57.710
In our IDE, we see
three important files--

00:11:57.710 --> 00:12:00.350
cloudbuild.yaml,
deployment.yaml, and the Docker

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:01.670
file.

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:04.340
Cloudbuild.yaml defines
a build pipeline.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.770
Here, you can see that
we've defined three steps--

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:11.640
Build Image, Push Image,
and Deploy to a Cluster.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.150
Next, deployment.yaml contains
our Kubernetes deployment

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:16.680
configuration.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.710
Here, we see that the Kubernetes
ServiceAccount, a Deployment,

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:23.850
and a HorizontalPodAutoscaler
have been defined.

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:26.370
Finally, the Docker
file shows what is

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:28.350
baked into our container image.

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.750
Here, we pull an
upstream nginx image,

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:34.990
write an awesome echo
statement, and then take a nap.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:36.880
To show Cloud Build
Hybrid in action,

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:39.478
we're going to increase
the sleep to 11 seconds

00:12:39.478 --> 00:12:41.020
and then commit and
push this change.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:59.580
Now that code is committed,
we'll switch back

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:02.010
to the GCP console, where
we see four new builds

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:04.165
in this schedule.

00:13:04.165 --> 00:13:06.040
Each build corresponds
to one of the triggers

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:07.000
we showed earlier--

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:11.430
one for Google Cloud, AWS,
Azures, and on premises.

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:14.490
Importantly, both the
CI and the CD steps

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:16.650
are happening at the same
time within each cluster

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:17.340
using Tekton.

00:13:25.352 --> 00:13:26.810
Now that a few
minutes have passed,

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.518
we'll see that our builds
are starting to complete.

00:13:29.518 --> 00:13:31.560
Next, we're going to click
on one of these builds

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:32.727
to see what that looks like.

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:41.930
Here, we can see
three build steps--

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:43.680
build, push, and deploy--

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:45.140
and our build logs.

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:47.540
You can also see metadata
related to the build.

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:50.060
For example, we can see that
it ran on hybrid worker pool

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:56.867
gke-cluster-001.

00:13:56.867 --> 00:13:59.200
Finally, I want to show you
something else that's pretty

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:01.150
cool about Cloud Build Hybrid.

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:04.040
If you want to do a deep
analysis of your build logs,

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:06.915
these are already preintegrated
with Cloud Logging.

00:14:06.915 --> 00:14:08.290
This means that
all of your build

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:10.790
logs are already defined with
the powerful Common Expression

00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:11.710
Language.

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:13.960
You can modify as you
see fit and then use

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:15.975
to rerun your queries.

00:14:15.975 --> 00:14:17.850
This allows you to easily
dive in and explore

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:19.683
any complex build errors
you might run into.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:38.027
Now that our builds
are finished,

00:14:38.027 --> 00:14:39.860
I want to show you one
more important thing.

00:14:45.307 --> 00:14:47.140
When we explore the
build for another cloud,

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.690
like AWS, we see that
the build interface

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:52.840
looks identical to the
one we just saw for GKE,

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:54.451
which is totally awesome.

00:15:01.685 --> 00:15:04.060
Now it's time to check the
stats of the workloads we just

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:06.380
deployed.

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:08.450
In the Google Cloud
console, we see a screen

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:10.685
that shows what workloads
existed in the default

00:15:10.685 --> 00:15:13.242
Kubernetes namespace
of our clusters

00:15:13.242 --> 00:15:14.450
before we committed new code.

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:20.320
Hitting Refresh shows we
now see four new nginx

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:24.040
apps running across
Google Cloud, AWS, Azure,

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:26.760
and on premise.

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.583
And just for fun,
let's confirm what

00:15:28.583 --> 00:15:30.750
the dashboard for a few
other cloud providers shows.

00:15:35.570 --> 00:15:38.900
Hitting refresh in EKS shows
the same workload deployed

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:39.920
about five minutes ago.

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:48.090
The view from the AKS dashboard
tells the exact same story.

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:51.296
Same workload again-- this
time, it's four minutes old.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:15:59.330
And now we've reached
the end of our demo.

00:15:59.330 --> 00:16:00.890
We've seen how
Cloud Build Hybrid

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:03.920
can be used to both
build and deploy apps

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:06.830
across multiple environments
using Tekton-powered Kubernetes

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:09.410
awesomeness and
slick Google tooling.

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:11.485
Enjoy.

00:16:11.485 --> 00:16:13.610
DAMITH KARUNARATNE: Thanks
for the great demo, Ben.

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:15.830
To quickly recap,
you just saw Ben

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:18.680
show how Cloud Build Hybrid
provides a unified CI/CD

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:21.560
experience that abstracts
away the complexities

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:25.070
of cloud-native CI/CD with
Kubernetes while providing

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:29.450
the flexibility to build, test,
and deploy to any environment.

00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:31.430
Google Cloud's fully
managed control planed

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:34.250
provides a single pane of
glass to gain visibility

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:36.320
into all your
workloads, regardless

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:38.260
of where they're running.

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:40.930
Finally, Cloud Build
Hybrid harnesses

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:43.270
the power of
Kubernetes and Tekton

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:45.220
to run your workloads
reliably at scale.

00:16:47.750 --> 00:16:50.060
All of this will be
available in public preview

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:51.890
later this quarter.

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:54.620
In the meantime, to learn
more about Cloud Build,

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:57.290
take a look at the Cloud
Build documentation.

00:16:57.290 --> 00:16:58.910
And don't forget
to check out some

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:02.210
of the other sessions on
Cloud Build, Cloud Deploy,

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:05.520
and securing your software
delivery pipeline.

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.800
Thanks for tuning in and
learning about Google Cloud's

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:10.050
approach to CI/CD anywhere.

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:13.010
Enjoy the rest of
Google Cloud Next.

